.TH UTMP 4
.SH NAME
utmp, wtmp \- login records
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "#include <utmp.h>"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fButmp\fR file allows one to discover information about who is currently using VENIX. The file is a sequence of entries with the following structure declared in the include file:
.nf
struct utmp {
        char    ut_line[8];     /* tty name */
        char    ut_name[8];     /* user id */
        long    ut_time;        /* time on */
};
.fi
.PP
This structure gives the name of the special file associated with the user's terminal, the user's login name, and the time of the login in the form of
.BR time (2).
.PP
The \fBwtmp\fR file records all logins and logouts. Its format is exactly like \fButmp\fR except that a null user name indicates a logout on the associated terminal. Furthermore, the terminal name '~' indicates that the system was rebooted at the indicated time; the adjacent pair of entries with terminal names '|' and '}' indicate the system-maintained time just before and just after a \fBdate\fR command has changed the system's idea of the time.
.PP
\fBwtmp\fR is maintained by \fBlogin\fR(1) and \fBinit\fR (section 8, \fIInstallation and System Manager's Guide\fR). Neither of these programs creates the file, so if it is removed record-keeping is turned off. It is summarized by \fBac\fR(1).
.SH FILES
/etc/utmp
/usr/adm/wtmp
.SH "SEE ALSO"
login(1), init(8), who(1), ac(1)
.sp
